-- ======================================================================
--  Immersive Quest Markers: task-KIND map spots
-- ======================================================================
--  Puts a skull on a mutant hunt's PDA pin and turns a bounty's reticle red,
--  while the job is still outstanding, and puts the task's own marker back the
--  moment it is done and owed to somebody.
--
--  WHY THIS IS A MODULE AND NOT A COLOUR. A map spot's art and tint are attributes
--  on its <texture> element in map_spots.xml, read once when that file is parsed.
--  There is no runtime tint on a map spot and no per-task one -- every task sharing a
--  location TYPE shares its appearance -- so the only way to say "this pin is a mutant
--  hunt" is to point that task at a different type. iqm_map_spots.xml declares the two
--  new types; this file decides which tasks wear them.
--
--  HOW IT HOOKS: it doesn't. CGameTask::ChangeMapLocation is remove-then-recreate and
--  fully re-entrant (GameTask.cpp:126-135), and set/get/change_map_location are all
--  bound to Lua (GameTask_script.cpp:41-59), so a poller that compares wanted against
--  actual and calls change_map_location on the difference needs no monkey-patch, no
--  unlocalizer and no override of task_objects.script. That is rung 0 of the hook tree
--  and it is available here only because the engine happens to expose the setter; the
--  alternative was wrapping CGeneralTask:check_task, which every other task mod also
--  wants to wrap.
--
--  WHAT IT COSTS, and the one visible artefact. Vanilla re-applies a task's OWN spot
--  whenever the target changes (task_objects.script:219), so after a target change our
--  type is clobbered until the next pass -- up to SYNC_MS of vanilla icon. That is not
--  worth chasing, because the target change that matters is the one at hand-in, where
--  vanilla's answer and ours agree: the objective is done, task_kind has gone nil, and
--  the vanilla marker is exactly what should be there. The blips are on the changes
--  nobody is looking at.
--
--  THE SAVE HAZARD, and how it is closed. A task's location type STRING is written into
--  the save (SLocationKey::save, map_manager.cpp:62) and rebuilt through CMapLocation's
--  constructor on load, which R_ASSERTs on a type map_spots.xml does not declare
--  (map_location.cpp:105) -- an engine fatal that no pcall catches. Left alone, that means
--  a save taken while a bounty is flagged red will not load with this mod removed.
--
--  It is not left alone. revert_for_save (below) puts every flagged task back on its own
--  type inside the engine's pre-save callback, fourteen lines before the registry is
--  serialised, so NO SAVE EVER CONTAINS ONE OF THESE NAMES and the mod can be deleted at
--  any moment. Three further guards back that up:
--    * the type names are never handed out unless the DXML splice that declares them
--      actually PUT THEM IN THE DOM (see `declared`), so an install without the modded
--      exes' DXML support, or with a stale copy of iqm_map_spots.xml, gets vanilla spots
--      instead of a crash;
--    * the feature refuses to run at all without marshal, since that is what the pre-save
--      callback rides on (see apply_config);
--    * switching the feature off REVERTS every task still wearing one of ours, rather
--      than just ceasing to apply new ones -- which is why the pass below runs even
--      when the feature is off, and why it cannot be short-circuited on a flag that
--      does not survive a save. This is also the manual escape hatch: untick the option,
--      save, and the save is clean whatever else has gone wrong.
--  Personal Adjustable Waypoint carries the same hazard for its own ~100 types and does
--  not close it, so this is stricter than the pack's normal rather than looser.
-- ======================================================================

-- Kind -> location type. The type names are declared in
-- gamedata/configs/ui/iqm_map_spots.xml and spliced into map_spots*.xml by
-- modxml_n_iqm_map_icons; the keys are iqm_scan.task_kind's vocabulary.
local SPOT = {
	mutant = "iqm_task_mutant",
	bounty = "iqm_task_bounty",
	-- THERE WAS A `hostage` KEY HERE UNTIL R2.55, the bounty's mark in azure, sharing its
	-- atlas cell and differing only in tint. It is gone because iqm_scan now answers "bounty"
	-- for the rescue functor outright: a rescue and a contract turned out to differ only in
	-- the verb, and the verb is the part the player does not act on - either way the job is
	-- to kill the people at that pin. The reasoning is at STATUS_KIND in iqm_scan.script;
	-- reinstating it means a key here, a type in ui/iqm_map_spots.xml, and a pointer.
	--
	-- The envelope. Its stage 1 target is the person you are taking the package to, which
	-- is the same shape of mark as the two above and reads as the same reticle without a
	-- glyph to separate them. See iqm_task_delivery in ui/iqm_map_spots.xml.
	delivery = "iqm_task_delivery",
	-- THERE WERE TWO HOLLOW TYPES HERE UNTIL R2.62 -- `waypoint` and `open`, the pin that
	-- lands on a mark the map already draws. Both are gone with the coverage rule that fed
	-- them (see iqm_scan.task_kind): a task now covers what it points at, which is vanilla.
	-- iqm_beacon keeps BEACON_ICON.waypoint -- that is offer_waypoint's glyph, a separate
	-- feature with its own switch, and was never reached through this table.

	-- HAND-IN: objective done, the marker has moved onto the task giver, go and collect.
	--
	-- The one kind here that is a state rather than a job, which is why iqm_scan answers it
	-- ahead of everything else and never caches it -- see handin_state there. The others are
	-- properties of the section and latch for the session; this one has to be able to turn
	-- back off, and it does when the task completes and the location goes away.
	--
	-- IT REPLACED A BORROWED MARKER. Until R2.58 this state was drawn by
	-- gamma-active-task-ui-enhancements, which set atue_return_task_location itself while
	-- this mod merely re-textured it. That arrangement looked fine and had a hole in it:
	-- uninstall ATUE and the marker disappeared, because the meaning lived over there and
	-- only the picture lived here. Both live here now. The re-texturing stays in
	-- modxml_n_iqm_map_icons and is still correct for anyone running ATUE -- with it
	-- installed its spot wins, since OURS below deliberately refuses to take another mod's
	-- pin, and the player sees the same green diamond either way.
	handin   = "iqm_task_handin",
}

-- The reverse set, and it is what makes this safe to run alongside other task mods.
-- A task's spot is only ever touched when it currently carries either the type the
-- TASK ITSELF declares (t.spot -- i.e. nobody has moved it) or one of ours (i.e. we
-- moved it). Anything else means another mod owns that pin right now -- ATUE's
-- return-task spot is the live example in GAMMA -- and it is left exactly alone.
-- Without this the pass would happily overwrite ATUE's marker with a vanilla one on
-- its very first tick.
local OURS = {}
for _, v in pairs(SPOT) do OURS[v] = true end

-- PUBLISHED for modxml_n_iqm_map_icons, which probes the parsed DOM for every one of these
-- before it will let the declared flag go true. It used to probe a single representative
-- name, which passed on exactly the stale-XML case the probe exists to catch -- a deployed
-- iqm_map_spots.xml a version behind HAS the older types and not the newest. Handing the
-- list over rather than restating it there is what stops that recurring: this module is
-- the one that names these to change_map_location, so it is the one that knows them.
spot_types = SPOT

-- 750 ms. The upper bound on how long a task can wear the wrong pin after a target
-- change, and it only ever costs a glance at the PDA -- there is no frame-rate reason
-- to go faster, and no accuracy reason either, since nothing here is drawn in the
-- world. The pass itself is a table lookup and a string compare per ACTIVE task
-- (typically under thirty, even in GAMMA), so this is cheap enough to run
-- unconditionally, which the revert-on-disable promise above requires it to be.
local SYNC_MS = 750

local on = false        -- feature switched on AND the mod's map art in use
local next_t = 0
-- Bound in on_game_start: iqm_scan owns the classification and modxml_n_iqm_map_icons
-- owns the promise that the types exist. Both are read through their namespaces rather
-- than copied, because `declared` is only set when the engine first parses
-- map_spots.xml, which happens in game -- long after this file is loaded, and after
-- on_game_start too.
local task_kind, task_active = nil, nil

--- Pulled by iqm_core alongside every other module's, in the same order and for the
--  same reason: one read of the config per change, not one per tick.
--
--  GATED ON map_icons as well as its own key. The two new spots are IQM art whichever
--  way that switch is set -- they live in the mod's own spliced file, not in the
--  retexture pass -- so nothing technical forces this. It is a consistency call: a
--  player who has asked for vanilla map icons should not find an acid-green skull among
--  them. Turning map_icons off therefore reverts these live, without the restart the
--  retexture itself needs.
--  iqm_core.config(), NOT iqm_core.C. `C` is a file LOCAL in that module (`:649`), so
--  `iqm_core.C` is nil and every gate built on it is silently false -- which is exactly
--  what R2.48 shipped, and why neither the skull nor the red reticle ever appeared. The
--  accessor is what every other module uses (`iqm_beacon.apply_config:498`).
--
--  AND ON USE_MARSHAL, which looks unrelated and is not. The promise below -- that no save
--  ever contains one of our spot types -- is kept by the save_state callback, and
--  alife_storage_manager only fires that callback when marshal is present
--  (`alife_storage_manager.script:162-170`). Without it there is no pre-save hook, so the
--  types would reach saves with no way back out. Refusing to flag at all is then the only
--  honest answer: the feature is worth less than the promise. In practice this is never
--  false -- marshal ships with the engine's lua_extensions and half of Anomaly's own save
--  path depends on it -- so the guard costs nobody the feature and makes the invariant
--  something that holds by construction rather than by luck.
function apply_config()
	local C = iqm_core and iqm_core.config and iqm_core.config()
	on = (C and C.enabled and C.map_icons and C.map_task_kinds and USE_MARSHAL) and true or false
	next_t = 0
end

--- One pass over the active tasks.
--
--  LIVENESS IS iqm_scan.task_active, and it is checked BEFORE get_map_location rather than
--  after. A task whose locations have been removed holds a null m_map_location, and
--  GetMapLocation_script hands that shared_str's c_str() straight to luabind
--  (GameTask.h:120) -- not a road worth walking to find out what it does.
--
--  R2.48 invented `t.status == "selected"` for this instead. iqm_scan already had the
--  answer and had written it as a NEGATIVE test -- not completed, not failed, not
--  reversed, and the actor still holds the task -- which is the shape it is because that
--  is how the field actually behaves. Two liveness tests for one question is one of them
--  being wrong, and it was this one.
local function sync()
	local tm = task_manager and task_manager.get_task_manager and task_manager.get_task_manager()
	local info = tm and tm.task_info
	if not info then return end

	-- Read per pass, never cached: the splice fires on the frame the engine first
	-- parses map_spots.xml, which can be any time after this module is loaded. Cached
	-- at on_game_start it would read nil for the whole session.
	--
	-- ONE HAZARD THIS FLAG CANNOT COVER, and it is a MOD-DEVELOPMENT one rather than a
	-- player one. map_spots.xml is parsed by several CUIXml instances, but the only one
	-- that decides whether a type EXISTS for the engine is g_uiSpotXml -- built once, on
	-- the first map location of the process, and freed at DLL detach. This callback cannot
	-- tell which instance it is patching. So if the deployed iqm_map_spots.xml gains a type
	-- while the game is ALREADY RUNNING, a later parse (the PDA map window's, say) sets
	-- this flag true while g_uiSpotXml -- built earlier, from the older file -- has never
	-- heard of it, and the first change_map_location takes the game down. Loading a save
	-- does not help: it re-reads .script files but not map_spots.xml.
	--
	-- SO g_uiSpotXml HAS TO BE REBUILT, and R2.51 found that the engine can do that without
	-- the game being closed. This note used to say "restart, full stop", which was true of
	-- everything that had been tried and not true of the engine.
	-- CHUDManager::OnScreenResolutionChanged (HUDManager.cpp:464-482) does exactly the two
	-- things needed and in the right order: xr_delete(g_uiSpotXml), then
	-- Level().MapManager().ReloadSpots() (map_manager.cpp:309), which re-runs LoadSpot on
	-- every live location against the DOM the next parse builds. CXml::Load re-reads the
	-- file from the VFS and fires _G.COnXmlRead on the way past (xrXMLParser.cpp:143 ->
	-- ScriptXMLInit.cpp:55), so the DXML splice runs again and the new types are in the
	-- fresh DOM before any location asks for one.
	-- The trigger is a REAL resolution change, not vid_restart: seqResolutionChanged is
	-- processed only when the dimensions actually differ (Device_destroy.cpp:99-101 and
	-- 186-187), so an in-place vid_restart rebuilds nothing.
	-- The rule for a spot type added while the game is running is therefore: load a save to
	-- pick up the scripts, then flip the resolution and back to pick up the XML. Between
	-- those two this flag reads nil and the feature is simply OFF, which is the safe
	-- intermediate state rather than a hazardous one -- and it is why the flag being a
	-- namespace global that a script re-read clears is a feature and not an accident.
	local declared = modxml_n_iqm_map_icons and modxml_n_iqm_map_icons.iqm_task_spots_declared

	for _, t in pairs(info) do
		local gt = t.t
		local base = t.spot
		if gt and base and t.current_target and (not task_active or task_active(t)) then
			local cur = gt:get_map_location()
			-- Ours to move, or nobody's? Anything else belongs to another mod.
			if type(cur) == "string" and (OURS[cur] or cur == base) then
				local kind = (on and declared and task_kind) and task_kind(t) or nil
				local want = (kind and SPOT[kind]) or base
				if want ~= cur then
					gt:change_map_location(want, t.current_target)
				end
			end
		end
	end
end

local function actor_on_update()
	local tg = time_global()
	if tg < next_t then return end
	next_t = tg + SYNC_MS
	sync()
end

--- NO SAVE EVER CONTAINS ONE OF OUR SPOT TYPES.
--
--  This is what makes the mod safe to remove at any moment, and it turns the save hazard
--  in the header from a documented risk into one that cannot occur. Every flagged task is
--  put back on its own type just before the game writes, so what lands on disk is a
--  vanilla name that any install can load -- with or without this mod.
--
--  THE ORDERING IS THE WHOLE TRICK, and it is verifiable rather than hopeful. The engine
--  calls alife_storage_manager.CALifeStorageManager_before_save at
--  alife_storage_manager.cpp:75, and serialises the map-location registry at
--  `registry().save(stream)` on line 89 -- fourteen lines later, same function, same
--  frame. `save_state` is fired from inside that Lua function (`:170`), so a revert here
--  is guaranteed to be in the data the engine is about to write. It covers BOTH carriers
--  of the string: SLocationKey (via `registry().save`) and CGameTask's own m_map_location
--  (via `objects().save`, line 88), because both are downstream of the callback.
--
--  Re-applied on the very next frame -- `next_t = 0` rather than waiting out SYNC_MS -- so
--  the pins are plain for one frame during an autosave and nobody sees it.
--
--  `on` is flipped rather than a second reverting routine being written: "put everything
--  back" is precisely what a pass with the feature off already does, and the alternative
--  is two code paths that have to agree about what "everything" means.
local function revert_for_save()
	local was = on
	on = false
	sync()
	on = was
	next_t = 0
end

function on_game_start()
	task_kind   = iqm_scan and iqm_scan.task_kind
	task_active = iqm_scan and iqm_scan.task_active
	RegisterScriptCallback("actor_on_update", actor_on_update)
	RegisterScriptCallback("save_state", revert_for_save)
end
